    {
      "id": "e7f3d971-9df9-46a2-9a97-82dd49a42d30",
      "name": "MCP Turns Tools Into Teammates",
      "path": "WWW / Why MCP Is Awesome / MCP Turns Tools Into Teammates",
      "docTypeId": "d3054eb2-9a6c-44cc-bfc9-b5f02379703c",
      "templateId": "d01f27e6-1492-4b1c-b1be-849fcd849c2d",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "MCP Turns Tools Into Teammates"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-01 09:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "Before MCP, every integration was a bespoke handshake. Now a server describes itself once and every assistant can use it — that shift is what makes tools feel like teammates."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "listingImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "05d1355f-92e0-484a-b54e-b0040b72f3cd",
              "mediaKey": "241e2a6e-ae1f-4697-a70e-e55cc59109f4",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "9f62a623-29eb-4d95-9ca8-4f15d3aeb1b0",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "dd831c99-4c5b-42a3-ba4b-d5997f7118f3",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "27d2723c-7669-48db-ab04-c67e7907448d",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "5fc17657-e991-4f33-89df-dcf5b7e108cd",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "b331764d-c903-4f97-a18f-3fe5048b2aed",
                "key": "9f62a623-29eb-4d95-9ca8-4f15d3aeb1b0",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Before Model Context Protocol, wiring an AI assistant to a real system meant a bespoke integration: a custom plugin format here, a vendor-specific tool schema there, none of it reusable. MCP replaces all of that with one open contract — a server describes its tools, resources and prompts once, and any compliant client can use them immediately.</p><p>That sounds like plumbing, but the effect is cultural. Teams stop asking <em>can we connect this?</em> and start asking <em>what should this expose?</em>. The integration tax drops to near zero, so small internal tools become first-class citizens of every assistant.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "8a210f7d-d727-487d-ab27-bc14d52a65d1",
                "key": "dd831c99-4c5b-42a3-ba4b-d5997f7118f3",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "Photo: Alina Grubnyak on Unsplash"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "bbfef942-c237-444a-bcfd-b3ebf1598cd3",
                        "mediaKey": "fa7305fb-f9f1-4961-94e1-0b59e8c8ce59",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "b331764d-c903-4f97-a18f-3fe5048b2aed",
                "key": "27d2723c-7669-48db-ab04-c67e7907448d",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>We saw this first-hand building this very site. The schema was modeled, the photos imported and the pages published over MCP — no bespoke code, no one-off scripts. Each capability added to the server made the next step cheaper instead of more complicated.</p><p>The lesson for product teams: treat every service you own as a candidate MCP server. The cheapest way to make your product AI-ready is not a new frontend, it is a well-described toolbox.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "f2659879-c11a-4796-9fc6-ce46ce3455be",
                "key": "5fc17657-e991-4f33-89df-dcf5b7e108cd",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "attribution",
                    "value": "The Umbraco.Bench blog"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "quote",
                    "value": "The teams that win the AI era will not be the ones with the most integrations, but the ones whose tools describe themselves."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "9f62a623-29eb-4d95-9ca8-4f15d3aeb1b0",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "dd831c99-4c5b-42a3-ba4b-d5997f7118f3",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "27d2723c-7669-48db-ab04-c67e7907448d",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "5fc17657-e991-4f33-89df-dcf5b7e108cd",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/mcp-turns-tools-into-teammates/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
